# Study plan: multinomial logistic regression vs. k-NN on OpenML Iris (id 61)

Owner: `story-repo` (plan). Built from the objective (`story-repo`#1), the brief
(`idea-repo` `brief.md` @ 2c2b79e, requirements R1–R16) and the feasibility judgement
(`idea-review-repo` `feasibility.md` @ e4b70d1). Where this plan and the objective disagree,
the objective wins. The delivered paths and their owners are in `assignment.json`.

Status of the items below: the **protocol** in §2 is the plan's pre-specified default. The analysis
owner (`experiment-repo`) owns validity of fits and uncertainty and may amend any setting; it
records the setting it used, and the reason for any change, in `report/results/` before the
results are read, not after. The plan does not presume the outcome of any comparison.

## 1. Questions and the claims they can support

| Q | Question | Estimand | Claim form (decided by the result, not in advance) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Q1 | How accurate is each method on Iris under 10x stratified 5-fold CV? | mean outer-fold accuracy per method over the 50 shared splits, 95% interval | "LR: a [l, u]; k-NN: b [l, u]" |
| Q2 | Do the two methods differ? | mean paired difference d = acc(LR) − acc(k-NN) over the same 50 splits, 95% interval | if the interval excludes 0: "LR more/less accurate by d [l, u]"; if it covers 0: "no difference resolved; d [l, u]" (not "equivalent") |
| Q3 | Is the computation reproducible across machines? | fold-level results of the full CV run as an Ibex CPU job vs. run locally | "Ibex node X; identical" or "differ: max abs difference …, in splits …" |

Descriptive, needed to read Q1–Q2: distribution of the k chosen by inner CV over the 50 outer
folds; number of splits with d > 0, d = 0, d < 0 (fold accuracy has granularity 1/30, so ties are
expected).

## 2. Pre-specified protocol (defaults; analysis owner confirms or amends, see above)

- **Data (R1).** OpenML data id 61 (`iris`, v1), downloaded from OpenML itself (ARFF
  `https://openml.org/data/v1/download/61/iris.arff` or Parquet), not a bundled copy. Record URL,
  download time, MD5 (OpenML lists `ad484452702105cbf3d30f8deaba39a9` for the ARFF) and SHA-256;
  assert n = 150, 4 numeric features, 3 classes × 50, no missing values. Download once, and use
  the *same file* (checksum verified) in the local and the Ibex run; compute nodes and
  `local-compute` may lack network.
- **Outer CV (R4).** 10 repetitions × stratified 5-fold, shuffled, one fixed seed per repetition
  (e.g. repetition r uses seed r, or `RepeatedStratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, n_repeats=10,
  random_state=S)` with S recorded). Each test fold has 30 points (10 per class). Both methods are
  fit and scored on exactly the same 50 splits; save the split assignment (test indices per
  repetition/fold) with the results so the pairing can be checked.
- **Preprocessing.** Standardisation fitted on the training part only, inside a pipeline, for both
  methods, applied identically in inner and outer loops.
- **Method A (R2).** Multinomial (softmax) logistic regression: L2 penalty, C = 1.0 fixed
  (library default), lbfgs, `max_iter` large enough that no convergence warning occurs (record
  any). Verify it is multinomial, not one-vs-rest, under the pinned library version. LR is not
  tuned: the objective tunes only k; the asymmetry is stated as a limitation, not corrected by
  adding a study.
- **Method B (R3).** k-NN, Euclidean distance, uniform weights. k chosen by inner stratified
  5-fold CV (shuffled, seed derived deterministically from repetition and fold) on the outer
  training set only (120 points; inner training sets 96), maximising mean inner accuracy over the
  grid k ∈ {1, 3, 5, …, 31}; ties broken by the smallest k; refit on the whole outer training set
  with the chosen k. Record the chosen k per outer fold.
- **Metric.** Accuracy on each outer test fold. Store per (repetition, fold): accuracy of LR, of
  k-NN, the difference, chosen k, and the per-sample test predictions of both methods.
- **Intervals (R5, R6).** Primary: corrected resampled t interval (Nadeau & Bengio 2003; for r×k
  CV, Bouckaert & Frank 2004): mean ± t(0.975, 49) · sqrt((1/50 + 30/120) · s²), s² the sample
  variance of the 50 fold values; applied to each method's fold accuracies and to the 50 paired
  differences. The interval method is named in the table caption and the text. Secondary, labelled
  and optional (results file; the paper uses them only if space allows): naive t interval over the
  50 folds and t interval over the 10 repetition means, to show how much the choice matters. If an
  interval bound exceeds 1, report it as computed and say so, or use a bounded method the analysis
  owner justifies — not a silent clip.
- **Seeds and environment.** All seeds fixed in code and written to the results. One pinned
  environment (lock file in `report/code/`) used on both machines; feasibility found Ibex login
  Python with scikit-learn 1.5.0 / numpy 2.1.2 and no scikit-learn locally, so pin the local side
  to the Ibex versions (or build the same locked environment on Ibex). Single-threaded BLAS
  (`OMP_NUM_THREADS=OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=MKL_NUM_THREADS=1`).

## 3. Reproducibility comparison (R7–R10)

- One script runs the *full* CV (both methods, all 50 splits, inner CV included) and writes the
  fold-level results file and a summary; the same script, data file, seeds and environment are used
  locally (`local-compute`) and as one Ibex CPU job (SLURM `batch` partition, CPU only).
- The job records, from inside the job: `$SLURM_JOB_ID`, `$SLURMD_NODENAME` and `hostname`, CPU
  model, Python and library versions, data checksum; log kept in `report/results/`.
- **Identity criterion, fixed before comparing:** the runs are "identical" iff the fold-level
  results (accuracies, chosen k, per-sample predictions) are equal element-wise and every summary
  number (means, interval bounds) is equal as a float. Otherwise report, per quantity, the maximum
  absolute difference and which splits differ. Either outcome is reported as found; a difference
  is not tuned away.
- Scheduling: the Ibex queue is shared and can be slow, so the Ibex job is submitted as soon as the
  code is fixed (tracked with `ht-resource track`), and the local run goes in parallel.

## 4. What the figure and table must carry (owner: `figtab-repo`; numbers from `report/results/`)

- **Table (one):** rows LR, k-NN, LR − k-NN; columns mean accuracy and 95% interval (primary
  method, named in the caption). The Ibex node and identical-or-not go in the table *or* the text,
  not both (paper-repo and figtab-repo settle which).
- **Figure (one):** supports Q1–Q2: the 50 per-split accuracies of both methods (paired) and/or the
  50 paired differences, with means and the primary 95% intervals marked and the zero line on the
  difference; discreteness (steps of 1/30) visible rather than hidden. Chosen-k distribution may be
  a small panel or a sentence in the text.

## 5. Report (owner: `paper-repo`; format: `venue-repo`)

- `report/report.pdf` ≤ 2 pages as a whole (references included; checklist omitted, per
  `venue-repo` `venue/REQUIREMENTS.md`), NeurIPS 2026 template in anonymous submission mode,
  compiled from `report/report.tex` with TeX Live 2026; exactly one figure and one table.
- Content: question (Q1–Q3), data and protocol (§2, stated briefly), results (table, figure, chosen
  k, ties), reproducibility (node name, identity outcome), limitations: tiny and easy dataset with
  near-ceiling accuracy; LR untuned while k is tuned (objective's design); the corrected interval
  is an approximation; CV estimates average over training sets (Bates et al. 2024); UCI vs. Fisher
  rows (one line at most).
- Every number in the text, table and figure is generated by committed code from
  `report/results/` (e.g. a summary JSON and generated `\newcommand` macros); no hand-typed numbers.

